  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nKDDC radar observations late Saturday evening show the decaying \nremnants of an elevated mesoscale convective system moving northeast \nacross our northern zones. This activity will pose little threat \nother than heavy rain and frequent lightning as it continues \nnortheast. Otherwise, northerly winds in the wake of an earlier cold \nfront passage will bring much cooler air equatorward overnight, with \nlows dropping into the upper 50s northwest to the upper 60s \nsoutheast.\n\nDaytime today, guidance indicates an upper level shortwave trough \ncurrently over the northern Rockies, as per latest water vapor \nsatellite imagery, will inch eastward onto the northern plains. At \nthe surface, northeasterly winds will persist across southwest KS as \nhigh pressure settles into the area, supporting a long-awaited break \nfrom the record-breaking heat as afternoon highs will range from the \nlow/mid 70s near I-70 to the low/mid 80s along the KS/OK border. By \nlate afternoon/early evening, HREF members suggest thunderstorm \ndevelopment is possible in the post-frontal upslope flow regime over \neast-central CO and west-central KS, moving southeast through the \novernight period over our western zones. Roughly 1000-1500 J/kg and \n30-35 kts of deep-layer shear will be in place, supporting a severe \nrisk with any convection that forms, with large hail and damaging \nwind gusts the primary threats.\n\nShort range ensembles maintain upper level westerly flow across the \ncentral plains daytime Monday between a pair of shortwave troughs \nover the northern plains/upper Midwest and the central CA coast. As \na result, southwest KS will remain under the influence of weak \nsurface high pressure and light northeasterly winds, both of which \nwill help reinforce the cool airmass over our area and promote \nafternoon highs in the mid 70s to low 80s. Thunderstorm activity \nwill also likely be muted given the lack of upper level forcing.\n\nTuesday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nweak upper level ridging over the southern plains and a convoluted \npattern elsewhere will engender a continuation of the cool, \nseasonable temperatures across the central plains with afternoon \nhighs in the mid 70s to mid 80s each day through at least the end of \nthe business week. Precipitation chances will also exist each day as \nthe NBM has at least some chance pops (25-54%) every day into the \nweekend, with the highest probability surrounding Wednesday and \nThursday.",
